# Animated Technical Education Website Skill

You are building a **premium, motion-first learning product** — not a blog with GIFs.
Every lesson should feel like a carefully directed explainer: clear narrative, visual
state machines, interactive controls, and zero decorative noise.

## When this skill applies

- Animated / interactive technical education sites
- Visual explainers for algorithms, systems, networking, databases, crypto, math
- "Teach X with animations" or "visual book / visual course" products
- Next.js + motion (Motion/Framer, GSAP, Rive, Canvas, WebGL) stacks

## Non-negotiable product principles

1. **Concept before chrome** — every animation teaches; no pure decoration.
2. **One idea per scene** — 15–45s of attention budget per beat.
3. **State is explicit** — lessons are finite state machines, not free-form DOM mess.
4. **Learner control** — play/pause, step prev/next, scrub timeline, speed, restart.
5. **Progressive disclosure** — start concrete, then generalize, then edge cases.
6. **Accessible motion** — honor `prefers-reduced-motion`; never rely on color alone.
7. **Performance** — animate `transform`/`opacity` primarily; keep INP/LCP healthy.
8. **Source of truth** — content lives in structured MDX/JSON; UI is a renderer.

## Research-backed motion stack (pick deliberately)

| Need | Prefer | Avoid for core lessons |
|------|--------|------------------------|
| UI + scene choreography in React | **Motion** (`motion` / framer-motion) | Random CSS spam |
| Complex timelines / scroll sequences | **GSAP + ScrollTrigger** | Nested setTimeout chains |
| Interactive vector characters / state machines | **Rive** | Heavy Lottie loops for pedagogy |
| Code-driven technical diagrams / math | **SVG + Motion**, **D3**, **Canvas** | Screenshot GIFs |
| 3D spatial systems (rare) | **Three.js / R3F** only when 3D is the point | 3D for everything |
| Exportable video explainers | **Remotion** (offline) or **Motion Canvas** | Video as only format |
| Diagram-first sketching | Design in Excalidraw/tldraw → implement as React/SVG | Shipping sketches as the product |

**Default web lesson stack (recommended):**
Next.js (App Router) + TypeScript + Tailwind + Motion + MDX + Zustand + KaTeX + CodeMirror (optional).

## Architecture (required shape)

```
app/
  page.tsx                    # marketing / catalog
  subjects/[slug]/page.tsx
  lessons/[slug]/page.tsx     # lesson shell + player
components/
  lesson/
    LessonPlayer.tsx          # transport: play, step, scrub
    SceneStage.tsx            # active scene viewport
    StepRail.tsx              # outline / progress
    CaptionBar.tsx            # narrator text for current beat
  diagrams/                   # pure visual components (props = state)
  ui/                         # buttons, cards, callouts
content/
  subjects/*.ts               # catalog metadata
  lessons/*.mdx or *.ts       # structured lesson scripts
lib/
  lesson-machine.ts           # step index, reduce, seek
  motion.ts                   # shared easings, durations, reduced-motion
  cn.ts
types/
  lesson.ts
```

### Lesson data model (canonical)

```ts
export type LessonStep = {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  /** Narration / caption shown under the stage */
  caption: string;
  /** Optional longer explanation (markdown) */
  body?: string;
  /** Duration hint for autoplay (ms) */
  durationMs?: number;
  /** Diagram component key + props for this beat */
  visual: {
    component: string; // e.g. "BufferPoolScene"
    props: Record<string, unknown>;
  };
  /** Optional callouts, highlights, code snippets */
  callouts?: Array<{ label: string; text: string }>;
  code?: { language: string; source: string; highlight?: number[] };
};

export type Lesson = {
  slug: string;
  title: string;
  subject: string;
  level: "intro" | "intermediate" | "advanced";
  summary: string;
  prerequisites?: string[];
  steps: LessonStep[];
};
```

Render lessons by **index into `steps`**. Never hardcode multi-scene logic inside page.tsx.

## Pedagogy patterns (use these)

### 1. Concrete → abstract
Show a single worked example first (one page, one frame), then generalize.

### 2. Compare & contrast
Split stage: wrong policy vs right policy (e.g. LRU flood vs ARC).

### 3. State machine lessons
Name states. Animate transitions. Show invariant after each transition.

### 4. Scrubbable timeline
Learner drags playhead; diagram is a pure function of `t` or `stepIndex`.

### 5. Interactive lab after watch
After 3–6 directed steps, unlock a playground with constrained knobs.

### 6. Check for understanding
One multiple-choice or predict-the-next-state question before advancing chapters.

## Motion craft rules (quality bar)

From production educational sites (Comeau-style interactive essays, scroll explainers):

- Prefer **action-driven** motion (enter snappy / exit softer) over symmetric fluff.
- Default easings: enter `easeOut`, UI hover quick (120–180ms), scene morph 300–600ms.
- Stagger related items 30–60ms — not more unless intentional.
- Use **layoutId** / shared layout sparingly for continuity of the same object across steps.
- Never animate layout thrash properties (`width`/`height`/`top`/`left`) when `transform` works.
- Always implement:

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  *, *::before, *::after {
    animation-duration: 0.01ms !important;
    transition-duration: 0.01ms !important;
  }
}
```

And in JS:

```ts
const reduce = window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches;
```

When reduced motion is on: jump to final visual state, keep captions/steps, disable autoplay.

## Visual design system (high quality)

- **Typography:** one display font (tight tracking for titles) + one readable body (Inter / Geist / Source Serif for long form).
- **Color:** dark stage + light content OR soft paper light theme; accent for *active* teaching objects only.
- **Grid:** stage ~16:10 or 16:9; captions below; step rail left or bottom on mobile.
- **Depth:** subtle borders, soft shadows; no glassmorphism soup.
- **Diagrams:** thick strokes, limited palette (3–5 colors), labeled arrows, consistent icon language.
- **Empty / loading:** skeleton of the stage, not a blank page.

## Content authoring workflow

1. **Outline** the misconception → truth → intuition → formal model → pitfalls.
2. Write **step captions** as if voice-over (≤ 2 sentences each).
3. Define **visual props per step** before coding animation.
4. Implement diagram as pure component `f(props)`.
5. Wire `LessonPlayer` to step index.
6. Add keyboard: `Space` play/pause, `←/→` step, `Home/End`.
7. Mobile pass: stage stacks; controls large hit targets.

## Implementation checklist (every lesson PR)

- [ ] Lesson loads from structured content (not only JSX dump)
- [ ] Play / pause / step / scrub works
- [ ] Keyboard accessible
- [ ] `prefers-reduced-motion` respected
- [ ] No layout shift on step change (reserve stage height)
- [ ] Captions make sense without animation
- [ ] Code samples (if any) syntax-highlighted
- [ ] Math (if any) via KaTeX
- [ ] Lighthouse-friendly: lazy heavy diagrams, `dynamic(..., { ssr: false })` when needed
- [ ] Subject appears in catalog with level + summary

## Agent workflow (Grok / Cursor / Claude)

When the user asks to build or extend the site:

1. Read this skill + `references/stack.md` + `references/lesson-patterns.md`.
2. Confirm subject + first lesson topic if missing (ask once, concisely).
3. Scaffold or extend Next.js App Router project (do not reinvent if project exists).
4. Add/extend types + lesson content first.
5. Build or reuse diagram components.
6. Wire player shell.
7. Run `npm run build` (or project equivalent) and fix errors.
8. Summarize what was taught and how to run `npm run dev`.

### Cursor-specific
- Prefer editing with project rules in `.cursor/rules/animated-tech-edu.mdc`.
- Keep diffs focused; match existing component style.

### Claude-specific
- Follow `CLAUDE.md` project instructions; prefer small verifiable steps.

### Grok-specific
- Use this skill via `/animated-tech-edu`; create files with absolute paths when scaffolding.

## Anti-patterns (reject these)

- Autoplaying infinite loops that teach nothing
- Wall of text beside a static image labeled “animation”
- Step buttons that remount the whole page
- Unlabeled boxes and arrows
- 3D scenes for 2D concepts
- Ignoring mobile or reduced-motion users
- Hardcoding lesson copy only inside components with no content layer

## Reference files

- `references/stack.md` — libraries, versions guidance, install commands
- `references/lesson-patterns.md` — scene recipes for CS/systems topics
- `references/quality-rubric.md` — ship / no-ship checklist

## Default first demo lesson

If the user has no topic yet, ship a **Buffer Pool: Pages vs Frames** micro-lesson (pages, frames, page table, pin, dirty, eviction) with 5–7 scrubbable steps — it exercises diagrams, state, and transport controls.
